AI-Guided Symbolic Optimization of Noisy Intermediate-Scale Quantum Circuits

Author: Ouadi Maakoul

## 1. Introduction and Problem Statement

**Core Problem.** Let a target unitary $U$ be given as a quantum circuit on $n$ qubits, together with a hardware specification consisting of a native gate set $\mathcal{G}$, a coupling map $C$, and calibration data yielding, for every gate $g$ acting on qubit(s) $q$, a measured error probability $p_g(q)$. We seek a hardware-compatible circuit $U'$ such that

$$\|U - U'\|_{\diamond} \le \epsilon, \qquad U' \text{ uses only } \mathcal{G} \text{ and respects } C, \qquad \min_{U'} \mathbb{E}[\text{error}(U')],$$

where $\|\cdot\|_{\diamond}$ is the diamond norm and the expectation is taken over the device noise model.

The landscape of approaches to this problem has shifted dramatically in 2025–2026. On one side, purely symbolic methods — ZX-calculus simplification via PyZX, VOQC, and TKET — have well-understood completeness properties for Clifford+T but fail to generalize to arbitrary rotation gates or to exploit hardware topology during optimization. On the other side, purely neural approaches such as AlphaTensor-Quantum require separate training per circuit family and do not provide formal equivalence certificates. While AlphaTensor-Quantum has demonstrated superior performance on benchmark quantum arithmetic circuits, its applicability has been restricted to specific circuit families, requiring separate, time-intensive training for each new application.

More recently, RL-on-ZX methods have pushed the frontier: after training on small Clifford+T circuits of 5 qubits and a few tens of gates, PPO-trained GraphSAGE agents consistently improve the state of the art for at least up to 80-qubit, 2100-gate circuits, while remaining competitive in terms of computational performance. However, these methods still operate on a fixed, hand-crafted rule set and are therefore fundamentally bounded by the quality of the rules given to them. Concurrently, LLM-driven circuit design has shown genuine promise but fragile reliability: off-the-shelf models cannot partition circuits using popular few-shot approaches, and acceptable performance is only achieved after careful fine-tuning — the best accuracy of 53.4% is obtained by fine-tuning the open-source Llama-3.1 70B model.

**Our thesis** is that a principled integration of three components — *LLM-driven symbolic rule discovery with formal verification*, *ZX-RL search with hardware-adaptive extraction*, and *compilation-aware approximation with guaranteed error budgets* — can outperform any one of these paradigms alone, while remaining provably sound by construction.

